KE_FINDINGS = "KENYA (KE) - findings for cl-stack-calendars#45\n\nSources: every Kenya Gazette issue listed by Kenya Law for 2013-2024 (about 2,300 PDFs) was downloaded from the gazettes.africa mirror\n(archive.gazettes.africa, the Laws.Africa copy of the official Government Printer PDFs) and text-searched for 'PUBLIC HOLIDAYS ACT' /\n'Assumption of the Office of the President Act'. 2000-2012: only windows around Idd-ul-Fitr, elections and October were fetched, and the\narchive is very sparse before 2013 (Kenya Law lists 15 issues for 2000, ~80-130 a year 2001-2012). 2024-11 to 2026: new.kenyalaw.org sits\nbehind an AWS WAF challenge (HTTP 202); only gazettes that the Wayback Machine holds as source.pdf could be read (those cited by python-holidays).\nStatute text: Cap. 110 versions 2020-12-11 (PDF) and 2024-04-26 (HTML) from Kenya Law via Wayback; Act 18 of 2018 via WIPO Lex.\n\nIssue item 5 (KE Sunday rule): it is s.4, not s.3 (s.3 is the CS's power to add/substitute days). Text: 'Where, in any year, a day in Part I of\nthe Schedule falls on a Sunday, then the first succeeding day, not being a public holiday, shall be a public holiday and the first-mentioned day\nshall cease to be a public holiday' (Act 2 of 1984, from 18 May 1984). Covers every Part I day incl. Easter Monday (never Sunday) and Idd-ul-Fitr;\nSaturdays are not moved; gazetted s.3 days (Idd-ul-Azha, ad hoc) are not covered. Christmas on Sunday -> Tuesday 27 Dec (confirmed by GN 10683/2016\nand GN 15800/2022). 2021-12-13 and 2022-05-02 follow from s.4 (no notice needed); several in-lieu Mondays were gazetted anyway (2013-10-21,\n2014-06-02, 2016-05-02, 2017-01-02).\n\nIssue item 6 (10 October eras), corrected:\n- Moi Day: added by Act 9 of 1990 (from 14 Sep 1990; '1988' in the issue is not supported by the statute history). Observed 1990-2009.\n- 2010-2017: still in the Schedule but NOT observed (government practice after the 2010 Constitution, whose art. 9(4) lists only Madaraka,\n  Mashujaa and Jamhuri as national days). High Court JR 292 of 2017 ([2017] eKLR, KEHC 2017/2485, 6 Nov 2017) declared the omission illegal.\n- 2018-2020: observed as Moi Day (2019 and 2020 marketed as 'Huduma Day' - no gazette notice; the Schedule still said Moi Day).\n- Utamaduni Day: Act 20 of 2020 (from 11 Dec 2020) -> 2021, 2022, 2023.\n- Mazingira Day: Act 3 of 2024 (assent 24 Apr 2024, Kenya Law version 26 Apr 2024) -> FROM 2024, not 2025. Both the issue text ('Utamaduni\n  2021-2024') and python-holidays (Utamaduni 2024) are wrong for 2024.\n- Kenyatta Day -> Mashujaa Day: celebrated as Mashujaa from 2010 (Constitution art. 9(4)); Schedule text changed only by Act 18 of 2018\n  (from 18 Jan 2019), which also renamed 'Independence Day' to 'Jamhuri Day'. python-holidays switches the name in 2011.\n- Madaraka Day 1 June is in the Schedule throughout (python-holidays only has it from 2011 - wrong for 2000-2010).\n\nIssue item 6 (Idd-ul-Azha): confirmed - it is in Part II (holiday only for Muslims, s.2(2)). A nationwide holiday is gazetted only in some years:\n2016-09-12, 2018-08-21, 2019-08-12, 2020-07-31, 2021-07-20, 2022-07-11, 2023-06-28, 2024-06-17, 2025-06-06, 2026-05-27 (news).\nNo notice in 2013, 2014, 2015, 2017 (full-year scans). The starter's every-year Eid al-Adha is wrong for those years.\n\nIdd-ul-Fitr gazetted dates: 2009-09-21, 2010-09-11 (Sat), 2012-08-20, 2013-08-09, 2014-07-29, 2015-07-18 (Sat), 2016-07-07, 2017-06-26,\n2018-06-16 (Sat, statutory) + 06-15 extra, 2019-06-05, 2020-05-25, 2021-05-14, 2022-05-03, 2023-04-21, 2024-04-10, 2025-03-31, 2026-03-20 (news).\n2000-2008 and 2011: no notice found -> estimates (secondary).\n\nAd hoc days found (not in python-holidays unless noted): 2005-11-21 referendum and 2005-11-22; 2007-12-28 and 2007-12-31 (election week);\n2010-08-04 referendum; 2010-08-27 promulgation; 2013-04-09 inauguration; 2013-12-13 and 2013-12-14 (Jamhuri@50); 2015-11-26 Pope (p-h has it);\n2017-08-08, 2017-10-25, 2017-10-26, 2017-11-28; 2018-06-15; 2020-02-11; 2022-04-29, 08-09, 09-13; 2023-11-13; 2024-05-10; 2024-11-01;\n2025-10-17. python-holidays' Queen Elizabeth mourning days 2022-09-10..12 are not public holidays (no notice).\n\nConfidence: 2013-2024 primary (full-year gazette scan; 2024 Nov-Dec incomplete); 2025 primary but possibly incomplete (only notices with an\narchive copy); 2026 secondary (news reports giving the gazette notice numbers); 2005, 2007, 2009, 2010, 2012 primary for the notices found,\n2000-2004, 2006, 2008, 2011 secondary (statute + estimated Idd-ul-Fitr). 2027: no data -> omitted (rules: KE_FIXED + s.4; Mazingira Day Sunday\n2027-10-10 -> 10-11, Jamhuri Sunday -> 12-13, Boxing Sunday -> 12-27).\n"


